In Linux, there are several ways to take backups and restore data. The most common methods are cp, tar, rsync, scp, dd, and LVM snapshots.

cp is used to copy files or folders.
tar is used to create a backup archive and compress files.
rsync is used for incremental backups and syncing data between servers.
scp is used to copy backup files securely to another Linux server over SSH.
dd is used to create a complete backup of a disk or partition.
LVM snapshots are used to take a backup of a live logical volume without stopping applications.

In my work, I have mainly used tar for creating backups and scp for transferring backup files. I also understand how rsync works for efficient incremental backups.
